#!/usr/bin/env luajit
--
-- refuse-directory-change
--
-- A gate that sits in front of every shell command Claude Code tries to run and
-- refuses the ones that would move the working directory somewhere else.
--
-- The reason it exists: the working directory belongs to the person at the
-- terminal, not to the assistant. When a command walks off to another folder,
-- every later command in the session inherits that move, and the prompt, the
-- status line, and the person's own sense of where they are all quietly
-- disagree with reality. Nearly every real need for it has a flag instead --
-- git takes -C, make takes -C, tar takes -C, and most other tools accept a
-- full path -- so refusing the move costs almost nothing and removes a whole
-- class of surprise.
--
-- The refusal can be lifted one time, by the person and only by the person, by
-- creating the permission token named below. The gate spends the token on the
-- next directory change and deletes it, so a permission granted once is not a
-- permission granted forever.
--
-- Asking for confirmation instead was tried first and does not work: under the
-- "auto" permission mode the confirmation is resolved without ever reaching a
-- human, which would leave this gate looking present while doing nothing. A
-- refusal is honoured; a request is not. Hence a refusal plus a token.
--
-- HOW IT READS A COMMAND
--
-- Through the shared shell-word reader (libs/shell-command-scan.lua), which
-- splits the line the way a shell does. So the gate sees the real command word
-- behind `if ...; then cd x; fi`, `builtin cd`, `command cd`, `FOO=1 cd`,
-- `\cd`, `"cd"`, `eval "cd x"`, `bash -c 'cd x'`, and `cd>/dev/null x`, and it
-- does not see `cd` inside a quoted string or a heredoc written to a file. The
-- regular expression it used before missed the first list and refused the
-- second.
--
-- A `cd` inside a child shell -- `(cd x && make)`, `$(cd x && pwd)`,
-- `bash -c 'cd x'` -- cannot move the session's directory, and is refused
-- anyway. Partly habit: a command that reaches for cd in a subshell reaches for
-- it bare the next time. And partly because the difference is not cheap to
-- prove from text: `( cd x ); make` and `cd x; make` differ by two characters.
-- `git -C` and `make -C` do the same job and leave nothing to judge.
--
-- OBSERVED ON CLAUDE CODE 2.1.280: the harness appears to drop a leading
-- `cd <the current directory> &&` from a command before PreToolUse hooks see
-- it, so that no-op shape passes live even though this script refuses it when
-- fed the text directly (and the test suite checks exactly that). It is not in
-- the changelog. To tell a stripped no-op from a broken gate, try the change
-- inside a subshell -- `(cd /tmp && pwd)` -- which the harness leaves alone and
-- this gate refuses.
--
-- WHAT THIS IS NOT
--
-- This is not a security boundary and must never be mistaken for one. The
-- assistant it constrains can read and write this file, can delete the hook
-- entry that installs it, and can create the token itself. Any of those defeats
-- it completely, and none of them is difficult. `source ./script` and a script
-- piped into a shell are not read at all.
--
-- What it buys is not prevention. It is that the ways around it are all things
-- a person would see: a token being touched, this script being edited, settings
-- being rewritten. An honour system with a witness, not a lock.
--
-- MOVING WHEN ALLOWED (scripts issue 041, 2026-10-18)
--
-- The permission file may be empty (any one move) or hold a folder (a move
-- to that folder only: `! echo /new/place > /tmp/claude-allow-cwd-change`).
-- Folders are compared by where they really are, so a permission written
-- with a folder's old name allows a move to its new name and the reverse. A
-- named permission does not let a move elsewhere through, and is kept for
-- the move it was made for.
--
-- A permission lives PERMISSION_MINUTES. One older than that is removed the
-- next time this gate sees a move, the refusal says so, and it is not
-- honoured. Why: the harness drops some moves before any hook sees them
-- (below), so a permission given for a move that never reached the gate
-- stayed armed, and the next move the session tried, for any reason, would
-- have spent it. That is what happened on 2026-10-18: the owner moved a
-- project to another disk, left a link at the old name, and asked the
-- session to move from the old name to the new; the move was dropped and
-- the permission lay there unspent.
--
-- A move to the folder the session is already in, by another name, changes
-- only the name the session goes by (its prompt, its status line, the paths
-- it writes). It needs a permission like any other; with one, it goes through
-- in one step and the person sees a notice saying it was a change of name.
-- (A two-step version, out to the new name's parent and back in, was built
-- and tried on 2026-10-18 and taken out the same day: the parent was outside
-- the session's allowed folders, where Claude Code resets the shell, and the
-- name that stuck was never Claude Code's record anyway but the PWD each
-- command's shell inherits from the `claude` program -- which
-- carry-working-folder-name now carries.) Every move must be its own command:
-- Claude Code drops a leading `cd DIR &&` when DIR is the current folder,
-- comparing real places, so `cd /new/place && pwd` never reaches this gate.

-- Reads the pending command as JSON on standard input. Says nothing when the
-- command is fine; prints a refusal when it is not; prints a visible warning
-- (and lets the command run) when it cannot read its input; prints a notice
-- to the person (and lets the command run) when a permission was spent on a
-- change of name of the folder the session is in.
--
-- Usage: refuse-directory-change [token-path] [scripts-directory]

local TOKEN = arg[1] or "/tmp/claude-allow-cwd-change"
-- How long a permission lives (issue 041, open question 1; ten minutes until
-- the owner chooses): long enough to type the permission and then ask for the
-- move, short enough that a forgotten one does not wait for a later move.
local PERMISSION_MINUTES = 10
local DIR = arg[2] or "/home/ritz/programming/ai-stuff/scripts"
local GATE_NAME = "refuse-directory-change"

package.path = DIR .. "/libs/?.lua;" .. package.path
local gate = require("hook-gate")
local scan = require("shell-command-scan")

-- The command words that move the working directory.
local MOVES_DIRECTORY = { cd = true, pushd = true, popd = true }

-- {{{ local function find_directory_change()
-- The first command on the line whose real command word moves the directory
-- (as text, and as its words), or nil. Wrappers and assignments in front of it are stepped past by the
-- reader, so `builtin cd` and `FOO=1 cd` are cd.
local function find_directory_change(command_text)
    for _, command in ipairs(scan.all_commands(command_text)) do
        local words = scan.effective_words(command)
        -- a command with no command word (only assignments) moves nothing;
        -- one whose word moves the directory is the answer
        if words and MOVES_DIRECTORY[words[1]] then
            return table.concat(words, " "), words
        end
    end
    return nil
end
-- }}}

-- {{{ local function target_of()
-- The folder a move names, as written: the first word after the command word
-- that is not an option (`cd -P /x` is /x). `cd` alone is the home folder;
-- `cd -` and `popd` name a folder only the shell knows, so they give nil.
local function target_of(words)
    if words[1] == "popd" then return nil end
    for k = 2, #words do
        local w = words[k]
        if w == "-" then return nil end
        if w:sub(1, 1) ~= "-" then return w end
    end
    return os.getenv("HOME")
end
-- }}}

-- {{{ local function real_place()
-- Where a folder really is (every link followed), or nil when it does not
-- exist or cannot be read. A relative name is read from `cwd`, the folder
-- the session is in; `~` is the home folder.
local function real_place(name, cwd)
    if not name or name == "" then return nil end
    if name == "~" then name = os.getenv("HOME") or name
    elseif name:sub(1, 2) == "~/" then name = (os.getenv("HOME") or "~") .. name:sub(2) end
    if name:sub(1, 1) ~= "/" then
        if not cwd then return nil end
        name = cwd .. "/" .. name
    end
    local out, ok = gate.run({ "realpath", "-e", name })
    if not ok then return nil end
    return (out:gsub("%s+$", ""))
end
-- }}}

-- {{{ local function read_permission()
-- The permission file's state: "none", "expired" (and then it is removed),
-- "unreadable" (there, but not a file this gate can read and remove), or
-- "any" / "named" with the folder it names, as written.
local function read_permission()
    if not os.rename(TOKEN, TOKEN) then return "none" end
    local stat_out, ok = gate.run({ "stat", "-c", "%Y %F", TOKEN })
    local made, kind = stat_out:match("^(%d+) (.-)%s*$")
    if not ok or not made or kind ~= "regular empty file" and kind ~= "regular file" then return "unreadable" end
    if os.time() - tonumber(made) > PERMISSION_MINUTES * 60 then
        os.remove(TOKEN)
        return "expired", os.date("%H:%M", tonumber(made))
    end
    local f = io.open(TOKEN, "r")
    local text = f and f:read("*a") or ""
    if f then f:close() end
    text = text:gsub("^%s+", ""):gsub("%s+$", "")
    if text == "" then return "any" end
    return "named", text
end
-- }}}

-- {{{ local function allow_with_notice()
-- Lets the command run and tells the person why, as a notice (not a refusal).
local function allow_with_notice(json, message)
    io.stdout:write(json.encode({ systemMessage = GATE_NAME .. ": " .. message }), "\n")
    os.exit(0)
end
-- }}}

-- {{{ local function main()
local function main()
    local input, json = gate.read_input(GATE_NAME, DIR)
    local command_text = gate.pending_command(GATE_NAME, input)

    local offending, words = find_directory_change(command_text)
    -- nothing on the line moves the directory: almost every command ends here
    if not offending then os.exit(0) end

    local cwd       = type(input.cwd) == "string" and input.cwd or nil
    local written   = target_of(words)
    local target    = real_place(written, cwd)
    local here      = real_place(cwd, nil)
    -- the same folder by another name: the move changes only the name the
    -- session goes by
    local same      = target and here and target == here

    local reason = "This command changes the working directory (" .. offending .. "), which belongs to the user and does not move unless they specifically ask for it."
    if same then
        reason = reason .. " " .. written .. " is the same folder the session is in now, under another name (both are " .. target .. "): the move would change only the name the session goes by."
    end
    reason = reason .. " Do the work from where you are: git -C <dir>, make -C <dir>, tar -C <dir>, or absolute paths. A heredoc needs no directory change either -- git -C <dir> commit -F - reads from stdin perfectly well."

    local state, detail = read_permission()
    -- the folder a named permission is for, where it really is
    local allowed = state == "named" and real_place(detail, cwd) or nil

    if state == "expired" then
        reason = reason .. " A permission to move was there, made at " .. detail .. ", but it was older than " .. PERMISSION_MINUTES .. " minutes, so it expired and was removed without being used."
    elseif state == "unreadable" then
        reason = reason .. " (The permission " .. TOKEN .. " is there but is not a plain file this gate can read and remove, so it was not honoured.)"
    elseif state == "any" then
        local spent, problem = gate.spend_token(TOKEN)
        if spent then
            if same then allow_with_notice(json, "the permission was spent on moving to " .. written .. ", the same folder as before under another name.") end
            os.exit(0)
        end
        if problem then reason = reason .. " (" .. problem .. ")" end
    elseif state == "named" then
        if allowed and target and allowed == target then
            local spent, problem = gate.spend_token(TOKEN)
            if spent then
                if same then allow_with_notice(json, "the permission was spent on moving to " .. written .. ", the same folder as before under another name.") end
                os.exit(0)
            end
            if problem then reason = reason .. " (" .. problem .. ")" end
        else
            reason = reason .. " The permission waiting is for a move to " .. detail .. (allowed and (" (" .. allowed .. ")") or " (a folder that cannot be found)") .. ", not to " .. (written or "the folder this move names") .. (target and (" (" .. target .. ")") or "") .. ", so it was kept for that move."
        end
    end

    local example = written or "<folder>"
    reason = reason .. " If the user genuinely wants the directory moved, ask them to type: ! touch " .. TOKEN .. " (any one move, within " .. PERMISSION_MINUTES .. " minutes), or ! echo " .. example .. " > " .. TOKEN .. " (a move to that folder only). Then make the move a command of its own, `cd " .. example .. "` alone: Claude Code drops a leading `cd DIR &&` when DIR is the folder the session is in under another name, so a chained move never reaches this gate and never happens."
    gate.refuse(json, reason)
end
-- }}}

main()
